window.docContents[108].resolve({"/Tier-2___-definitions/Computational___Specifications___/Computational.Specifications.Assumptions.KeyInd1KeyReal/#file-Computational___Specifications___Assumptions___KeyInd1KeyReal":{"contents":"Paper › Computational/Specifications/Assumptions › KeyInd1KeyReal.leanTier 2 · definitions\n\nTier 2 · definitions used by the computational proof 2 headline theorems depend on it 47 of 104 lines trusted source ↗trust banner in the header: B2, trusted: definitions used by the statement; the closure computes B3 for the computational proofRead the definitions shown. Only the declarations that the headline statements use are shown, with the namespace, section, open and variable commands needed to read them. The collapsed runs are theorems, which Lean checks, and definitions that no headline statement uses.\n\nKey indistinguishability with one-key identities and the real key derivationTrusted: definitions used by the statement. The CLI statements use pqxdhKeyIndImpl1KeyReal, pqxdhKeyInd1KeyExpForHandshake, pqxdhKeyInd1KeyRealExp, and KeyInd1KeyRealAdvantage, and Lemma 1 (Security/HeadlineDelta.lean) uses pqxdhKeyIndImpl1KeyReal and pqxdhKeyInd1KeyExpForHandshake via KeyInd1KeyRo.lean. Read with Computational/Specifications/Pqxdh/Game.lean (pqxdhKeyIndImpl). See Computational/docs/TRUSTED_BASE.md and Computational/docs/DEVIATIONS.md (DEV-CLI16).Whereas pqxdhKeyIndImpl1KeyRo (KeyInd1KeyRo.lean, bounded by Lemma 1) idealizes hkdfDerive96 as a lazily sampled random oracle (HkdfRO, DEV-PQ14), this file defines the real one-key handshake game pqxdhKeyInd1KeyRealExp using concrete P.hkdfDerive96 and libsignal's single-scalar X25519/XEdDSA identities (as in bhDrvKeyInd at useRo = false in Computational/Specifications/Game/StatementBinaryHarnessCore.lean). The CLI statements state clause 1 directly against KeyInd1KeyRealAdvantage and relate clause 2 to KeyInd1KeyRoAdvantage via KeyIndRoGap.Both one-key games start from KeyIndState.empty, disable .revealDhSecrets (which belongs to the harvest-now-decrypt-later game), and evaluate classical session freshness (isKeyIndInitStillFresh and isKeyIndRespStillFresh with flag true, as in allTestedSessionsFresh in the CLI game).\n\n\n\ndef pqxdhKeyIndImpl1KeyRealL45–69 ↗#Key-indistinguishability oracle with single-scalar X25519/signing identities and real HKDF derivation (P.hkdfDerive96). .initPrin u shares one sampled scalar across X25519 and signing, .roQuery evaluates P.hkdfDerive96, .revealDhSecrets returns .noneResp, and all other queries delegate to pqxdhKeyIndImpl false S P.\n\ndef pqxdhKeyInd1KeyExpForHandshakeL71–92 ↗#One-key key-indistinguishability experiment over handshake oracle ki: runs A from KeyIndState.empty S secb and returns a uniform coin if any tested session fails classical freshness (flag true), or b' == secb otherwise.\n\n2 declarations not shown (lines 94–105): theorems, which Lean checks, or definitions that no headline statement usesSignalPqxdh.pqxdhKeyInd1KeyRealExp, SignalPqxdh.KeyInd1KeyRealAdvantage\n\n\n\n","context":"AlphaProof Code: Verifying Signal’s Security at the Machine-Code Level","header":"Computational/Specifications/Assumptions/KeyInd1KeyReal.lean","id":"/Tier-2___-definitions/Computational___Specifications___/Computational.Specifications.Assumptions.KeyInd1KeyReal/#file-Computational___Specifications___Assumptions___KeyInd1KeyReal"}});